\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Rd}

%\VignetteEngine{knitr::knitr}
%\VignetteIndexEntry{Correlation and Covariance}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}
%\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}

\title{Correlation and Covariance using cor\_cov}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the \texttt{cor\_cov} function from the numTheory package.
The function computes both correlation and covariance between two numeric vectors.
Correlation measures the strength of linear association, while covariance measures
the joint variability of two variables.

\section{Inputs}
The function takes two numeric vectors, \texttt{x} and \texttt{y}, as input.

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{x} and \texttt{y} are the data vectors whose relationship is being analyzed.
\item The output includes both correlation and covariance values.
\end{itemize}

\section{Basic Example}
Here we compute correlation and covariance for two simple vectors:

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
library(numTheory)

x <- c(1,2,3,4,5)
y <- c(2,4,6,8,10)

# Correlation and covariance of x and y
cor_cov(x, y)
@

\section{Interpreting Results}
In this example, the correlation is 1 because y is exactly proportional to x.
The covariance is positive, reflecting that both variables increase together.

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
# Perfect proportionality: y = 2x
x <- c(1,2,3,4,5)
y <- 2 * x
cor_cov(x, y)
@

\section{Edge Cases}
The function also handles cases where vectors have zero variance or mismatched lengths.

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
# Zero variance in y
y2 <- c(5,5,5,5,5)
cor_cov(x, y2)

# Mismatched lengths (expected: error)
y3 <- c(1,2,3)
try(cor_cov(x, y3))
@

\section{Summary}
The \texttt{cor\_cov} function provides a convenient way to compute correlation and
covariance together, making it easy to analyze relationships between variables.

\end{document}
